shareinputdevices: send the pointer barrier position as (iiii), not ai

InputCaptureSession::setUpBarrier built the SetPointerBarriers payload with two
wrong D-Bus signatures:

  * `barrier_id` was the int literal `1`, so QVariant(int) marshalled it as `i`.
    The portal spec types it `u`.
  * `position` was QVariant::fromValue(QList<int>{...}), which marshals as `ai`
    -- an array. The portal spec types it `(iiii)`, a fixed-arity struct.

`(iiii)` and `ai` are distinct D-Bus signatures with different wire encodings,
and Qt performs no implicit conversion between them, correctly. A portal that
validates the payload therefore rejects every barrier. Observed against
xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland, which logs

  [WARN] [input-capture] invalid barrier payload:
    [System.Error.ENXIO] Failed to enter a variant (No such device or address)

and returns the barrier id in `failed_barriers` -- the spec-defined channel for
denied barriers. A dbus-monitor capture of the live call confirms the client
side:

  dict entry( string "barrier_id"  variant  int32 1 )
  dict entry( string "position"    variant  array [ int32 0, int32 0,
                                                    int32 0, int32 1079 ] )

The practical effect is that no pointer barrier is ever armed, so crossing a
screen edge never triggers input capture and the feature is silently inert.
There is no user-visible error; the plugin logs "Failed barriers" at info level
and carries on. Measured on one machine: zero barriers applied across the entire
uptime.

The geometry itself is correct (0,0 -> 0,1079 for a vertical barrier down the
left edge of a 1080p output, including the deliberate QRect::bottom() handling),
so this is purely a marshalling defect.

Fixed by building the struct explicitly with QDBusArgument::beginStructure(),
and by making the barrier_id literal unsigned. An inline QDBusArgument is used
rather than a registered metatype to keep the change within one function and
avoid introducing a new public type; a qDBusRegisterMetaType-based struct would
also work if that is preferred.

Reproduced on KDE Connect 26.04.3 with xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland b653ab53.
Not tested against xdg-desktop-portal-kde, whose Qt-based demarshalling may be
lenient enough to accept `ai` -- which would explain why this went unnoticed.

